ts":[{"text":"பரிசில்","lemma":"பரிசில்","gloss":"gift"}],"gloss":"gift"},{"surface":"அல்கலும்","parts":[{"text":"அல்கலும்","lemma":"அல்கல்","gloss":"every day"}],"gloss":"every day"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"தண்புனல்","parts":[{"text":"தண்புனல்","lemma":"தண்புனல்","gloss":"cool water"}],"gloss":"cool water"},{"surface":"வாயில்","parts":[{"text":"வாயில்","lemma":"வாயில்","gloss":"gateway"}],"gloss":"gateway"},{"surface":"துறையூர்","parts":[{"text":"துறையூர்","lemma":"துறையூர்","gloss":"Tuṟaiyūr"}],"gloss":"Tuṟaiyūr"},{"surface":"முன்றுறை","parts":[{"text":"முன்றுறை","lemma":"முன்றுறை","gloss":"front ghat/shore"}],"gloss":"front ghat/shore"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"நுண்","parts":[{"text":"நுண்","lemma":"நுண்மை","gloss":"fine"}],"gloss":"fine"},{"surface":"பல","parts":[{"text":"பல","lemma":"பல","gloss":"many"}],"gloss":"many"},{"surface":"மணலினும்","parts":[{"text":"மணலினும்","lemma":"மணல்","gloss":"more than the sand"}],"gloss":"more than the sand"},{"surface":"ஏத்தி","parts":[{"text":"ஏத்தி","lemma":"ஏத்துதல்","gloss":"praising"}],"gloss":"praising"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"உண்குவம்","parts":[{"text":"உண்குவம்","lemma":"உண்ணுதல்","gloss":"we will eat/enjoy"}],"gloss":"we will eat/enjoy"},{"surface":"பெரும","parts":[{"text":"பெரும","lemma":"பெருமான்","gloss":"O Lord"}],"gloss":"O Lord"},{"surface":"நீ","parts":[{"text":"நீ","lemma":"நீ","gloss":"you"}],"gloss":"you"},{"surface":"நல்கிய","parts":[{"text":"நல்கிய","lemma":"நல்குதல்","gloss":"granted"}],"gloss":"granted"},{"surface":"வளனே","parts":[{"text":"வளனே","lemma":"வளம்","gloss":"wealth"}],"gloss":"wealth"}]}],"literal":"Resembling the back of a lute's wooden body / on the many-stitched garment intertwined with threads / clinging to the gaps and not letting go of their grip / with crowds of nits crowded in the seams / shall I say the enemy called lice is one? / Due to not eating, our flesh withered / eyes filled with clear tears / along with my many relatives who are grieved / shall I say the enemy called hunger that afflicts us is one? / Knowing such a state, those who do not give / saying 'give what is yours' so that our standing falters / on the mountain slope dense with abundant trees / like monkeys, the lowly short soldiers / shall I say the enemy that spreads and afflicts us is one? / In that manner, Āy is the one who knows all our afflictions / thinking thus and praising your name / with my mouth full, trusting your fame / climbing the desert burned by the sun's heat / we came here with great desire / those who give to us give to others / those who give to others give to themselves, saying / all that I have spoken / considering what is fitting to you / please grant and release the gift, so that every day / at the front ghat of Tuṟaiyūr with its gateway of cool water / more than the many fine grains of sand, praising you / we shall enjoy, O Lord, the wealth you granted!","translation":"Resembling the curved back of a lute, our ragged clothes are held together by countless threads and stitches. Clinging to every gap and crease, crowds of nits and lice infest them without letting go. Shall I call this army of lice our first enemy? Due to starvation, our flesh has withered away, and our eyes are filled with clear tears. Along with my many grieving relatives, we are constantly tormented by hunger. Shall I call this hunger our second enemy? When we beg from ungenerous patrons who know our plight, asking them to give what they have, our dignity falters. On the mountain slopes dense with trees, forest bandits who look like short monkeys attack and plunder us. Shall I call these bandits our third enemy? Knowing that you, Āy, are the one who understands and relieves all such afflictions, we praised your name. Trusting your fame with all our hearts, we crossed the scorching desert burned by the sun and arrived here with great hope. I have spoken of how those who give to others ultimately enrich themselves. Now, considering what is fitting for you, please grant us your gifts and let us depart. Every single day, at the front ghat of Tuṟaiyūr with its cool waters, we shall praise you for times outnumbering the fine sands, and enjoy the wealth you have granted, O Lord!","modern":"யாழின் மரத்தாலான பின்புறத்தைப் போன்ற, நூல்களால் தைக்கப்பட்ட பல தையல்களை உடைய கந்தல் ஆடையில், இடைவெளிகளைப் பற்றிக்கொண்டு விடாமல் பிடித்துள்ள ஈர்களின் கூட்டத்தோடு, தையல் மடிப்புகளில் பெருகியுள்ள பேன் என்னும் பகை ஒன்று என்று சொல்வேனோ? உணவில்லாததால் உடம்பு வாடி, தெளிந்த கண்ணீர் கண்களில் நிறைய, வருத்தமுற்ற என் பல சுற்றத்தாருடன் எங்களைப் பசியால் வருத்தும் பகை ஒன்று என்று சொல்வேனோ? எங்களின் இத்தகைய வறுமை நிலையை அறிந்தும் கொடை தராத உலோபிகளிடம் 'உன்னிடமுள்ளதைத் தா' என்று இரந்து கேட்பதால் எமது மானம் அழிய, மரங்கள் நிறைந்த குளிர்ந்த மலைச்சரிவில் குரங்குகளைப் போன்ற சிறிய உடலமைப்பைக் கொண்ட கொடிய வேடர்கள் வழிப்பறி செய்து எங்களை அலைக்கழிக்கும் பகை ஒன்று என்று சொல்வேனோ? இத்தகைய துன்பங்கள் அனைத்தையும் போக்கவல்லவன் ஆய் அண்டிரன் என்று கருதி, உனது பெயரைப் போற்றி, வாய் நிறைய உனது புகழை நம்பி, கதிரவன் சுட்டெரிக்கும் பாலைவனக் கடந்து, உன்னிடம் பெரும் ஆசையோடு வந்துள்ளோம். 'எமக்குக் கொடுப்பவர்கள் பிறருக்கும் கொடுப்பவர்கள்; பிறருக்குக் கொடுப்பவர்கள் தமக்கே கொடுத்துக் கொள்கிறார்கள்' என்று நான் கூறினேன். உனக்குத் தகுதியானதை நீ ஆராய்ந்து, பரிசிலை வழங்கி எங்களை அனுப்பி வைப்பாயாக. குளிர்ந்த நீர் பாயும் வாயிலைக் கொண்ட துறையூரின் முன்றுறையிலுள்ள நுட்பமான பல மணல் துகள்களைக் காட்டிலும் பல காலம் உன்னைப் புகழ்ந்து பாடி, பெருமானே, நீ வழங்கிய வளத்தை நாங்கள் உண்டு மகிழ்வோம்.","explanation":"The poet Tuṟaiyūr Ōṭai Kiḻār addresses the generous chieftain Āy Aṇṭiraṉ in this poem of the pāṭāṇ tiṇai, under the theme of requesting gifts (paricil kaṭānilai). The poet describes three distinct 'enemies' or afflictions that plague him and his kin: the lice and nits that infest their ragged, many-stitched clothes resembling the back of a lute; the agonizing hunger that withers their bodies and fills their eyes with tears; and the monkey-like forest bandits who harass them on the mountain paths when they are forced to beg from ungenerous patrons. Trusting in Āy's legendary fame as a savior who understands all such miseries, the poet has crossed the scorching desert to reach him. He reminds the chieftain that those who give to others ultimately enrich themselves through virtue and fame. Finally, the poet requests a fitting gift so that he and his relatives can return to their fertile hometown of Tuṟaiyūr and praise the patron's name for days outnumbering the fine sands of the river ghat.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}